  "not_computable_from_the_release": [
    "Review submission bodies and PR-level comments carry no parent identifier, so no exact addressed edge can be constructed for them at any threshold. The out-of-scope share is a hard measurement boundary of the release, not a tuning choice.",
    "GitHub review threads are one level deep in this release, so a reply anchor always names a thread root; the reply-to-a-reply case does not exist here and cannot be studied."
  ],
  "interpretation": "On the complete cross-product trigger cohort (8608 PRs, outputs/cross_agent_review/cross_feedback_response_chains.parquet), 31507 of 73491 reviewer-side review interactions (42.9%) are inline review comments, the only modality carrying a reply anchor; the remaining 41984 events are review submission bodies or PR-level comments and admit no exact parent edge. Within inline comments, 81.3% are thread roots, which is the ceiling on what an exact edge can attach to. At the cohort level, 4824 of 8608 (56.0%) cross-product review triggers are inline and therefore in scope; the remaining 44.0% are structurally invisible to the addressed edge. Restricting to the RQ3 estimation cohort of PRs still open at the 48-hour landmark (1733 PRs), the same quantities are 42.7% inline reviewer-side events, 81.2% thread roots, and 61.6% inline triggers in scope; that cohort is more inline-heavy and must not be quoted as a property of the study population. The coarse PR-level proxy is a descriptive upper bound in both cohorts and supports no estimate.",
  "scope": "descriptive coverage audit; no causal claim and no estimate"
}